(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-2-ophthalmology-i-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Common Ophthalmological Disorders Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 2","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 29-year-old man is diagnosed with episcleritis. Which is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["Artificial tears and an oral non-steroidal anti-inflammatory taken with food","Systemic corticosteroids started immediately at diagnosis","Topical antibiotic drops four times daily","Urgent same-day ophthalmology referral"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and the anti-inflammatory should be taken with food. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man has begun treatment for a corneal ulcer. What should he be told about follow-up and healing?","choices":["He needs review in one month, and it should heal within a year","He needs review in two weeks, and it will not heal without surgery","He needs review the next day, and most ulcers heal over two to three weeks","He needs no follow-up, and it should heal in 48 hours"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — next-day follow-up after starting an anti-infective, with a two to three week course. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has a sectoral patch of redness in one eye with mild ache, no discharge and no photophobia. After topical anaesthesia the redness moves slightly when nudged with a cotton-tip applicator. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Anterior uveitis","Episcleritis","Scleritis","Bacterial conjunctivitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — mobile vessels, mild pain and no photophobia point to the superficial layer. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 17-year-old has bilateral itchy red eyes every spring with swollen lids and stringy watery discharge. Vision is normal. Which single topical agent both blocks histamine and stabilises mast cells?","choices":["Artificial tears alone","Olopatadine","A topical fluoroquinolone","A topical corticosteroid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — olopatadine carries both actions in one drop. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 21-year-old woman who sleeps in her contact lenses has severe unilateral eye pain, photophobia and blurred vision. The cornea is hazy so the iris details cannot be seen clearly, and there is a ring of redness at the corneal edge that does not spare the limbus. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Bacterial keratitis","Viral conjunctivitis","Subconjunctival haemorrhage","Episcleritis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — corneal haze with ciliary flush in a lens overwearer. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman is anxious about the dye for fluorescein angiography because she reacted to contrast for a scan once. What is accurate to tell her?","choices":["It is injected into the eye itself","Fluorescein contains no iodine and is relatively safe","It contains iodine, so her previous reaction is a contraindication","It is radioactive and requires shielding"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — fluorescein is iodine-free, so a contrast reaction does not carry over. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man started treatment for scleritis three days ago. His eye still looks just as inflamed, but his pain is much better. How should this be interpreted?","choices":["Treatment has failed and should be changed immediately","The diagnosis was wrong and this is episcleritis","He is at imminent risk of scleral perforation and needs surgery now","This is the expected first sign of response — decreased pain comes before any change in appearance"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — pain settles first, so an unchanged-looking eye is not failure. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man has had a red left eye with stringy mucoid discharge for six weeks. Topical antibiotics have not helped, and the lower palpebral conjunctiva shows follicles (pale bumps that are redder at the base). Which test confirms the diagnosis?","choices":["Slit lamp examination with fluorescein staining","Computed tomography of the orbits with contrast","Conjunctival scraping for potassium hydroxide preparation","Conjunctival nucleic acid amplification testing"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the chronic course, the follicles and the failure of topical therapy point to chlamydial disease. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man has a painful red eye with a ring of redness at the corneal edge (ciliary flush) and pain in that eye when light is shone into the OTHER eye (consensual photophobia). His pupil is small and irregular. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Oral antivirals for ten days","Urgent referral to ophthalmology within 24 hours for slit lamp and dilated fundoscopy","Topical antibiotic drops with a review appointment in 48 hours","Reassurance and artificial tears"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — anterior uveitis, where delayed diagnosis may cost vision. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman has a tender red swelling at the inner corner of her lower eyelid, with purulent material expressible from the lower punctum. She is afebrile, systemically well and reliable. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Outpatient oral antibiotics for ten days with warm compresses","Hospital admission for intravenous antibiotics for 48 to 72 hours","Topical antibiotic drops alone","Immediate surgical dacryocystorhinostomy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — mild dacryocystitis in a well, reliable patient is managed as an outpatient. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man's acute dacryocystitis has settled on antibiotics. What should he be told about what comes next?","choices":["No further action is needed now that the acute infection has cleared","He will need lifelong daily antibiotics","He will need annual computed tomography scanning","Probing and irrigation are often needed to assess whether the tear drainage system is open, and surgery may follow"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the underlying obstruction still has to be addressed. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 32-year-old man has a tender lid margin nodule, and the surrounding lid and periorbital skin are now diffusely swollen, red and warm. The globe is white with full painless movements. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Continue warm compresses alone for another two weeks","Refer for immediate incision and drainage of the nodule","Start topical antibiotic drops","Treat as pre-septal cellulitis with systemic antibiotics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — an associated pre-septal cellulitis takes that pathway. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has a swollen, red, tender left eyelid and fever after sinusitis. The globe is white, the pupil reacts normally, and eye movements are full and painless with normal vision. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pre-septal (periorbital) cellulitis","Post-septal (orbital) cellulitis","Dacryocystitis","Allergic conjunctivitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a white globe with full painless movements and normal vision places the infection in front of the septum. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man has pain, redness and swelling confined to the outer third of his upper eyelid, with an enlarged node in front of the ear on the same side. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pre-septal cellulitis","Hordeolum","Dacryoadenitis","Dacryocystitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the lacrimal gland sits superotemporally, and preauricular nodes are described. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman needs the front of her eye examined in magnified detail — the lids, cornea, conjunctiva, sclera and iris. Which instrument is designed for this?","choices":["A tonometer pen","A Wood lamp","A slit lamp","A direct ophthalmoscope"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a low-power microscope with a high-intensity slit beam for the anterior structures. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has an active herpes simplex epithelial dendrite. Which action should specifically NOT be taken by the primary care provider?","choices":["Staining the cornea with fluorescein","Prescribing topical glucocorticoid drops","Prescribing an oral antiviral","Referring for slit lamp examination"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — steroids over an active dendrite risk corneal melt, so that decision belongs to ophthalmology. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man may have a corneal abrasion after a fingernail injury. Which examination will best demonstrate it?","choices":["Inject fluorescein intravenously and photograph the retina with a blue flash","Instil phenylephrine and observe for blanching","Measure intraocular pressure","Instil fluorescein and examine under a Wood lamp with ultraviolet light"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the surface fluorescein examination detects abrasions, ulcers and foreign bodies. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman treated for bacterial conjunctivitis 24 hours ago returns with no improvement. She wears contact lenses daily. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Continue the same drops and review in one week","Switch to an oral antibiotic and review in 48 hours","Add a topical corticosteroid to settle the inflammation","Urgent referral to ophthalmology"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — lens wear and failure to improve in 24 hours are both urgent-referral criteria. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has two days of mild ache and a sectoral patch of redness in one eye, with no discharge and no photophobia. Which bedside test best confirms the diagnosis?","choices":["Measure the intraocular pressure with a tonometer pen","Obtain computed tomography of the orbits with contrast","Instil 2.5 per cent phenylephrine and look for blanching of the vessels after 15 minutes","Instil fluorescein and examine under cobalt blue light for a corneal dendrite"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — episcleral vessels blanch; the mild painless sectoral redness fits episcleritis. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man has a painful red eye with photophobia. Fluorescein under cobalt blue light shows a branching corneal lesion with small knobs at the ends of each branch (terminal end bulbs). Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["A topical fluoroquinolone for ten days","Oral doxycycline for seven days","Oral antiviral therapy for ten days","Topical corticosteroid drops started today"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a true dendrite with terminal end bulbs is pathognomonic for herpes simplex. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 44-year-old fisherman has a fleshy triangular growth on the nasal conjunctiva of one eye that now extends a short distance onto the cornea. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Corneal ulcer","Chalazion","Pterygium","Pinguecula"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — corneal extension is what makes it a pterygium rather than a pinguecula. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man on warfarin notices a bright red patch on the white of one eye after a coughing fit. There is no pain, vision is normal, the pupil is normal and the cornea is clear. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Scleritis","Subconjunctival haemorrhage","Bacterial conjunctivitis","Anterior uveitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — Valsalva plus anticoagulation, and the eye is otherwise entirely normal. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis has severe deep boring eye pain that wakes her at night and radiates to her cheek. The sclera has a bluish-purple hue (violaceous discolouration, the choroid showing through thinned sclera). Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Topical antibiotic drops and review in one week","Reassurance that it is self-limited","Urgent referral to ophthalmology","Artificial tears and an oral non-steroidal anti-inflammatory, review in two days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the sclera is at risk of perforation and may need a surgical patch. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman presents 48 hours after a dermatomal vesicular rash appeared over her forehead and upper lid. Which is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["Reassure her that no treatment is needed for a dermatomal rash","Start an oral antiviral now and refer for slit lamp examination and dilated fundoscopy","Wait for ophthalmology to confirm ocular involvement before starting any antiviral","Start a topical corticosteroid to reduce inflammation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — within the ideal 72-hour window, and the eye still needs examining. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man has dacryoadenitis and the cause is not yet clear. Which action should be avoided?","choices":["Starting corticosteroids before bacterial and other infectious causes have been reasonably excluded","Giving paracetamol for pain","Reassessing him after 24 hours of empiric oral antibiotic treatment","Monitoring for signs of orbital involvement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — bacterial and neoplastic causes must be excluded before steroids. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 23 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"A 77-year-old woman with ectropion asks what will actually fix it. What is the most accurate answer?","choices":["Surgery is the definitive treatment; lubrication only protects the surface in the meantime","Lubricating ointment used consistently will gradually correct the lid position","It will resolve on its own within two to four weeks","A course of oral antibiotics will resolve it"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — only surgery restores lid position permanently. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 38-year-old contact lens wearer resists opening a painful watering eye. There is a ring of redness at the corneal edge (ciliary flush) and a white spot on the cornea with surrounding irregularity. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Urgent referral within 24 hours","Routine referral within two weeks","Reassurance and lubricating drops","Emergent referral to ophthalmology"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a corneal ulcer outranks keratitis's 24-hour window and goes emergently. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman has completed a two-week lid hygiene regimen for blepharitis with no improvement. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Try a topical antibiotic","Go straight to oral antibiotics","Refer urgently to ophthalmology today","Start a topical corticosteroid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the escalation runs lid hygiene, then topical antibiotics, then oral. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has blurred vision, floaters and a patch of missing vision in one eye, with no pain at all. Ophthalmology finds cells in the posterior vitreous and vitreous haze. Which statement about treatment is correct?","choices":["No treatment is required as it resolves spontaneously","Topical treatment will not work here — an intraocular corticosteroid injection may be required","Topical corticosteroid drops are the first-line treatment for this","Oral doxycycline for seven days is first-line"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — this is the key management difference from anterior uveitis. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman has had a lid nodule in the same spot for four months, and it has recurred twice in the past year. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Refer to ophthalmology to rule out sebaceous carcinoma","Continue warm compresses for another three months","Start oral antibiotics for two weeks","Reassure her that recurrence is normal and no action is needed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — recurrence, or persistence beyond two to three months, triggers this specific concern. — CMS I Common Ophthalmological Disorders 2026 - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"}]};
